Thinking: We need to answer user question: "How do I load a .pmtiles file as a source in MapLibre GL JS?" Provide step-by-step instructions, code snippets, mention installing pmtiles library, registering protocol, using url, handling CORS, example style, maybe React usage. Should be concise but thorough. Use skill content.

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** recipe for loading a `.pmtiles` file (vector, raster or raster‑DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` protocol, so we add a tiny protocol handler that knows how to fetch byte‑ranges from a static file.

```bash
# npm (or yarn / pnpm)
npm install pmtiles
```

> **Why this library?**  
> It registers a `pmtiles:` protocol that reads the archive’s header, builds a TileJSON on‑the‑fly, and serves each tile via HTTP Range requests. The rest of MapLibre works exactly as if you had a normal XYZ source.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (or any module that runs at app start)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create a protocol instance (it holds an internal cache of opened archives)
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Tell MapLibre to use it for the "pmtiles" scheme
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// OPTIONAL – clean‑up when the page is unloaded (helps with hot‑module‑reload)
window.addEventListener('unload', () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
});
```

> **Tip for React / SPA:** import this module in the top‑level component (e.g. `App.jsx`) or in a separate `setup()` function that runs only once. Do **not** call `addProtocol` inside a component that mounts/unmounts repeatedly.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the `.pmtiles` file

### 3‑a. Vector tiles (most common)

```js
// map initialization (anywhere after the protocol registration)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // ONE source that points at the whole archive
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // NOTE: use **url**, not **tiles**, so the protocol can read the header
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        // If the file is on the same origin you can also use a relative URL:
        // url: 'pmtiles://./data/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw water polygons from the "water" layer inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads from the "road" layer
      {
        id: 'road',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': [
            'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
            5, 0.5,
            12, 2,
            16, 6
          ]
        }
      }

      // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own source‑layer name
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### 3‑b. Raster imagery (PNG/JPEG tiles)

```js
// In the same style object, add a raster source:
sources: {
  rasterPmtiles: {
    type: 'raster',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/ortho.pmtiles',
    tileSize: 256               // default, change if your tiles are 512px
  }
},
layers: [
  {
    id: 'ortho',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'rasterPmtiles',
    paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
  }
]
```

### 3‑c. Raster‑DEM (elevation) for 3‑D terrain

```js
sources: {
  terrainPmtiles: {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/terrain.pmtiles',
    // Most DEM archives use the Terrarium encoding; change if yours is "mapbox"
    encoding: 'terrarium'
  }
},
terrain: {
  source: 'terrainPmtiles',
  exaggeration: 1.5
}
```

> **Important:** The `url` field (not `tiles`) lets the protocol read the archive’s header and automatically set `minzoom` / `maxzoom`. If you use a `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` template you lose that information and MapLibre will assume a default `maxzoom: 22`, causing unnecessary empty‑tile requests.

