## 4️⃣ CORS & HTTP Range support on the host

| Host | What you need to set |
|------|----------------------|
| **AWS S3 / Cloudflare R2** | `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. Enable public read or use signed URLs. |
| **GitHub Pages / Netlify / Vercel** | Same CORS headers; most static hosts already send `Accept-Ranges`. |
| **CDN (CloudFront, Cloudflare, etc.)** | Forward the `Range` header to the origin and cache the range responses. Set a long `Cache‑Control` on the `.pmtiles` file (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). |

If you get a **CORS error** in the console, add the header on the bucket or via your CDN’s edge rules.

---

## 5️⃣ Full Minimal HTML Example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; top:0; left:0; right:0; bottom:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create map with a vector PMTiles source
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/data/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'road',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 2 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, and MapLibre will fetch only the byte ranges it needs from `world.pmtiles`. No tile server, no extra backend.

