## TL;DR  

MapLibre GL JS v4 changed the **protocol API**:

| v3 (old) | v4 (new) |
|----------|----------|
| `maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (url) => data)` – sync, returns raw `ArrayBuffer`/`Uint8Array`/`string` | `maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abort) => Promise<ProtocolResponse>)` – async, returns a **Promise** that resolves to an object `{ data, cacheControl?, expires?, headers? }` (or just the raw `ArrayBuffer` for simple cases). |
| No `abort` signal handling | The second argument is an **AbortSignal** you must respect (or ignore). |
| The handler was called for **any** request that matched the protocol (including style JSON, glyphs, sprites, …) | Same, but the signature is stricter – the handler must return a **Promise** (or a value that can be wrapped in `Promise.resolve`). |

Because of that, a callback that simply returned a `Uint8Array` (or called `fetch(...).then(r=>r.arrayBuffer())` without returning the promise) now silently fails and MapLibre reports “Failed to load source …”.

Below is a **step‑by‑step migration guide** plus a ready‑to‑copy example for a **custom vector tile protocol** (the same pattern works for raster, raster‑dem, glyphs, sprites, etc.). At the end we also show how to keep the **PMTiles** example working – you only need to use the `pmtiles.Protocol` helper which already implements the new API.

---

## 1️⃣ What changed in the API

```js
// v3 (removed)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (url) => {
  // sync – return raw data or a string
  return mySyncTileFetcher(url);
});
```

```js
// v4 (current)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abortSignal) => {
  // async – return a Promise that resolves to the tile data
  // `params` = { url, type, ... } – see docs for the exact shape
  // `abortSignal` is an AbortSignal that will be aborted if the request is cancelled
  return myAsyncTileFetcher(params.url, abortSignal);
});
```

* **`params`** – an object with at least `{ url, type }`.  
  *`type`* is the source type (`'vector'`, `'raster'`, `'raster-dem'`, `'glyphs'`, `'sprite'`, …).  
  You can also read `params.headers` (the request headers MapLibre would send) if you need them.

* **Return value** – you can either:
  * Resolve to a **raw `ArrayBuffer` / `Uint8Array` / `string`** (MapLibre will treat it as the response body).  
  * Resolve to an **object** with the shape `{ data, cacheControl?, expires?, headers? }`.  
    * `data` must be an `ArrayBuffer`/`Uint8Array`/`string`.  
    * `cacheControl` / `expires` are optional HTTP‑style cache directives that MapLibre will forward to the browser.  
    * `headers` is an optional plain‑object of additional response headers (e.g. `{'Content-Type': 'application/x-protobuf'}`).

* **Abort handling** – If the request is cancelled (e.g. the user pans away before the tile finishes loading) MapLibre will call `abortSignal.abort()`. Your fetch logic should respect it, otherwise you’ll waste bandwidth. The simplest way is to pass the signal to `fetch`:

```js
fetch(url, { signal: abortSignal })
```

If you don’t care about aborts you can just ignore the second argument, but **you must still return a Promise**.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal “upgrade‑only” fix

If your old handler looked like this:

```js
// v3 – broken in v4
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (url) => {
  // synchronous or a fetch that you *didn't* return
  fetch(url).then(r => r.arrayBuffer());   // ❌ not returned!
});
```

Just **return** the promise (or wrap the sync value in `Promise.resolve`):

```js
// v4 – one‑liner fix
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abort) => {
  return fetch(params.url, { signal: abort }).then(r => r.arrayBuffer());
});
```

That’s all you need for the simplest case.

---

## 3️⃣ Full‑featured example (custom vector tiles)

Below is a complete, production‑ready snippet that:

* Registers a protocol named `mytiles`.
* Supports **cancellation** via the AbortSignal.
* Returns the proper response object (including `Content-Type` for MapLibre to decode protobuf tiles).
* Shows how to **remove** the protocol when you’re done (important for SPA clean‑up).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre v4 – custom protocol demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const protocolName = 'mytiles';

  // The handler receives (params, abortSignal) and must return a Promise.
  const handler = (params, abortSignal) => {
    // `params.url` is the full URL after the protocol prefix, e.g.
    //   mytiles://https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles
    // For a simple tile server you might have:
    //   mytiles://https://my.cdn.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
    // In that case you can replace the placeholders yourself:
    const url = params.url
      .replace('{z}', params.z)
      .replace('{x}', params.x)
      .replace('{y}', params.y);

    // Use fetch with the abort signal so the request can be cancelled.
    return fetch(url, { signal: abortSignal })
      .then(resp => {
        if (!resp.ok) {
          // MapLibre expects a rejected promise for HTTP errors.
          throw new Error(`HTTP ${resp.status}`);
        }
        // Return an object so we can set the correct MIME type.
        return resp.arrayBuffer().then(buf => ({
          data: buf,
          // Important for vector tiles – MapLibre needs protobuf.
          // For raster tiles you would use 'image/png' or 'image/jpeg'.
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-protobuf' }
        }));
      });
  };

  // Register the protocol (must be done *before* creating the map)
  maplibregl.addProtocol(protocolName, handler);

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Create the map, referencing the protocol in the style
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // The URL must start with the protocol name followed by `://`
        // and then a full HTTP(S) URL (or any other scheme you like).
        // MapLibre will pass the whole string to the handler.
        mySource: {
          type: 'vector',
          // Note: we use `url:` (not `tiles:`) so the protocol can read
          // the TileJSON header (minzoom/maxzoom) if the source is a PMTiles
          // archive. For plain tile URLs you can also use `tiles: []`.
          url: `${protocolName}://https://my.cdn.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        // add more layers that reference the same source…
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Clean‑up (optional but recommended for SPAs)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // When the map is removed (e.g. React unmount) you should deregister
  // the protocol to avoid memory leaks.
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol(protocolName);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Register protocol** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', handler)` – the handler follows the v4 signature. |
| **Build the URL** | The handler receives `params.url` (the part after `mytiles://`). We replace `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders ourselves. |
| **Abort support** | `fetch(..., { signal: abortSignal })` – if the tile request is cancelled, the fetch aborts automatically. |
| **Return shape** | We resolve to `{ data, headers }`. The `Content-Type` header tells MapLibre that the payload is protobuf vector tiles. |
| **Use in style** | The source URL is `mytiles://https://…/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`. MapLibre calls the protocol, which fetches the real tile. |
| **Clean‑up** | `maplibregl.removeProtocol('mytiles')` removes the handler when the map is destroyed. |

