## Adding 3‑D Terrain from a PMTiles Elevation Archive  
Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** recipe for loading a raster‑DEM PMTiles file (Terrarium or Mapbox‑encoded elevation tiles) into a MapLibre GL JS map and turning the map into a 3‑D terrain surface.

---

### 1. Prerequisites  

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (v2.x or later) | Core rendering library |
| **pmtiles** npm package | Registers the `pmtiles://` protocol so MapLibre can request byte‑ranges from a static file |
| **A PMTiles elevation archive** (`.pmtiles`) | Must contain raster‑DEM tiles (Terrarium or Mapbox encoding). You can create it with GDAL, tippecanoe, Planetiler, or download a ready‑made one (e.g. from Protomaps or Overture). |
| **Static host that supports HTTP Range & CORS** (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, etc.) | The client fetches only the byte range for the tile it needs. The host must return `Accept‑Ranges: bytes` and `Access‑Control‑Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Optional: a sprite & glyphs source** | Needed for any label layers you add on top of the terrain. |

---

### 2. Install the PMTiles library  

```bash
npm install pmtiles   # or yarn add pmtiles
```

> **Tip:** The library is tiny (≈ 30 KB gzipped) and has no other dependencies.

---

### 3. Register the `pmtiles` protocol **once** (e.g. in your app entry point)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (import this before any map is created)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create a protocol instance that knows how to fetch byte‑ranges
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// (Optional) expose a cleanup function if you ever need to remove it
export const removePmtilesProtocol = () => maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Do not** call `addProtocol` inside a component’s render loop – it must be done **before** the first `new maplibregl.Map()`.

---

### 4. Build the MapLibre style that references the elevation PMTiles

#### 4.1 Minimal style (JSON literal)

```js
const terrainStyle = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    // ---- 1️⃣ Vector basemap (any source you like) ----
    "basemap": {
      type: "vector",
      // Example: OpenStreetMap vector tiles hosted elsewhere
      url: "https://basemap.example.com/tiles.json"
    },

    // ---- 2️⃣ Elevation source (raster‑DEM) ----
    "elevation": {
      type: "raster-dem",
      // The URL can be either:
      //   • pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/elevation.pmtiles
      //   • https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/elevation.pmtiles   (no prefix)
      // The protocol handler will recognise the .pmtiles suffix and use range requests.
      url: "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/elevation.pmtiles",
      // Choose the encoding that matches your file:
      //   * "terrarium" – 8‑bit RGB where elevation = (R*256 + G + B/256) - 32768
      //   * "mapbox"   – 16‑bit signed integer stored in two bytes (big‑endian)
      encoding: "terrarium"
    },

    // ---- 3️⃣ (Optional) Sprite & glyphs for labels ----
    "sprite": {
      type: "image",
      url: "https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/sprite.png"
    },
    "glyphs": "https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"
  },

  // ---- 4️⃣ Layers that draw the vector basemap ----
  layers: [
    {
      id: "background",
      type: "background",
      paint: { "background-color": "#f0f0f0" }
    },
    {
      id: "water",
      type: "fill",
      source: "basemap",
      "source-layer": "water",
      paint: { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
    },
    // … add more vector layers (roads, labels, etc.) as you like …
  ]
};
```

#### 4.2 What the fields mean

| Field | Explanation |
|-------|-------------|
| `type: "raster-dem"` | Tells MapLibre that the source contains elevation data, not imagery. |
| `url: "pmtiles://…"` | The `pmtiles` protocol handler will fetch the file via HTTP Range. You can also omit the `pmtiles://` prefix; the handler will still work as long as the URL ends with `.pmtiles`. |
| `encoding` | Must match how the DEM values were encoded when the PMTiles file was created. **Terrarium** is the most common for DIY pipelines; **Mapbox** is used by the official Mapbox terrain tiles. |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | **Do not set these manually**. When you use `url:` (instead of `tiles:`) the protocol reads the header inside the PMTiles archive and automatically supplies the correct zoom range to MapLibre. |
| `terrain` property (added later) | This is where you *activate* the DEM for 3‑D rendering. See step 5. |

---

### 5. Create the MapLibre map and enable terrain

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import './pmtiles-setup.js';   // registers the protocol

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // <div id="map"></div> in your HTML
  style: terrainStyle,       // the JSON from step 4
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // lon, lat (example: San Francisco)
  zoom: 13,
  pitch: 60,                // tilt the camera so you can see 3‑D
  bearing: -30,             // optional rotation
  antialias: true           // improves hillshading quality
});

// **Activate the DEM** – this tells MapLibre to use the raster‑DEM source for terrain
map.on('load', () => {
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'elevation', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  // `exaggeration` > 1 makes mountains look taller; 1 is true scale.
});
```

#### 5.1 Adding a hillshade layer (optional but looks great)

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: "hillshade",
  type: "hillshade",
  source: "elevation",
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth) in degrees, 0 = north
    "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,
    // Light intensity (0‑1)
    "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "map",
    "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.5)",
    "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.3)"
  }
}, "water"); // insert below the water layer (or any other layer you prefer)
```

> **Why a separate hillshade layer?**  
> The `hillshade` type reads the same DEM source but renders a shaded relief image. It does **not** affect the 3‑D terrain itself; it’s purely visual.

---

### 6. Hosting the PMTiles file correctly  

| Platform | Steps to make it work |
|----------|----------------------|
| **AWS S3** | 1. Upload `elevation.pmtiles`. <br>2. Set **Object ACL** → *Public* (or use signed URLs). <br>3. Enable **CORS**: <br>```xml<br><CORSRule><AllowedOrigin>*</AllowedOrigin><AllowedMethod>GET</AllowedMethod><AllowedHeader>*</AllowedHeader></AllowedMethod></CORSRule>``` <br>4. Verify the object returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes` (S3 does this automatically). |
| **Cloudflare R2** | Same as S3 (R2 is S3‑compatible). Add a Cloudflare **Cache‑Everything** page rule if you want CDN caching. |
| **GitHub Pages** | File size limit = **100 MB** (uncompressed). Add a `CORS` header via a `static.json` or `netlify.toml` if you use Netlify as a proxy. |
| **Netlify / Vercel** | Upload the file as a static asset. Ensure `netlify.toml` contains `[[headers]]` with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. |
| **Any other static host** | Test with `curl -I -H "Range: bytes=0-0" https://host/elevation.pmtiles`. You should see `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and a `206 Partial Content` response. |

**Cache‑Control** – Because the file is immutable, set a long max‑age:

```
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
```

That way browsers and CDNs keep the file (and its range responses) forever.

---

### 7. Full Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles Terrain</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol (once)
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@2.5.0/dist/index.min.js';
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Define the style (inline for brevity)
  const style = {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: "vector",
        url: "https://basemap.example.com/tiles.json"
      },
      elevation: {
        type: "raster-dem",
        url: "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/elevation.pmtiles",
        encoding: "terrarium"
      }
    },
    layers: [
      { id: "background", type: "background", paint: { "background-color": "#f8f4f0" } },
      { id: "water", type: "fill", source: "basemap", "source-layer": "water", paint: { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" } }
    ]
  };

  // 3️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: "map",
    style,
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 13,
    pitch: 60,
    bearing: -30,
    antialias: true
  });

  // 4️⃣ Enable terrain when the style is ready
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: "elevation", exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // Optional hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: "hillshade",
      type: "hillshade",
      source: "elevation",
      paint: {}
    }, "water");
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Copy‑paste the above into a file (`index.html`) and open it in a browser (or serve it via any static host). The map will show the vector basemap draped over the 3‑D terrain derived from the PMTiles elevation file.*

---

### 8. Common Pitfalls & How to Fix Them  

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Map loads but terrain stays flat** | `elevation` source not recognized (wrong `type` or missing protocol) | Verify `type: "raster-dem"` and that `pmtiles` protocol is registered *before* the map is created. |
| **`setTerrain` throws “source not found”** | Source name typo or the source is defined after the map is created | Ensure the source key (`elevation`) matches exactly in both the style JSON and the `setTerrain` call. |
| **Tiles appear as blank (white) or missing** | CORS header missing on the host | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) to the bucket/object. |
| **Range requests return 416 (Requested Range Not Satisfiable)** | Host does **not** support byte‑range requests (e.g., some cheap static hosts) | Switch to a host that supports `Range` (S3, R2, Cloudflare, Netlify, Vercel). |
| **Terrain looks “stretched” or “compressed”** | Wrong `encoding` (Terrarium vs. Mapbox) | Re‑create the PMTiles with the correct encoding, or change the `encoding` property in the style to match the file. |
| **Performance is sluggish** | Large PMTiles file without CDN, or no caching headers | Put the file behind a CDN (CloudFront, Cloudflare) and set long `Cache‑Control` headers. |
| **Over‑zooming shows empty tiles** | Using `tiles: [...]` instead of `url:` in the source definition | Always use `url:` for PMTiles; the protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct `maxzoom`. |
| **Hillshade looks wrong (no shadows)** | Hillshade layer added *before* the DEM source is ready | Add the hillshade layer **after** the `load` event (or after `setTerrain`). |

---

### 9. Generating a Terrarium‑Encoded DEM PMTiles (if you need to create one)

```bash
# 1️⃣ Convert a raster DEM (GeoTIFF) to MBTiles with GDAL (requires GDAL ≥ 3.8)
gdal_translate -of MBTILES \
  -co TILE_FORMAT=PNG \
  -co COMPRESS=DEFLATE \
  -co ZLEVEL=9 \
  -co RESAMPLING=AVERAGE \
  -co BLOCKSIZE=256 \
  -co TILING_SCHEME=GoogleMapsCompatible \
  -co TILESIZE=256 \
  input_terrain.tif terrain.mbtiles

# 2️⃣ Convert MBTiles → PMTiles (preserves Terrarium encoding)
pmtiles convert terrain.mbtiles terrain.pmtiles
```

*If you already have a raster‑DEM MBTiles file, the `pmtiles convert` step is all you need.*

---

### 10. TL;DR Checklist  

1. **Host** a `.pmtiles` elevation file that uses Terrarium or Mapbox encoding.  
2. **Enable CORS** and **Range** support on the host.  
3. **Install** `pmtiles` npm package and **register** the protocol *before* creating any map.  
4. **Add a `raster-dem` source** pointing at the PMTiles URL (`pmtiles://…`).  
5. **Create the map**, then on `load` call `map.setTerrain({source: 'elevation', exaggeration: <num>})`.  
6. (Optional) **Add a hillshade layer** for visual relief.  
7. **Set long cache headers** and, if possible, serve the file through a CDN.  

You now have a fully‑static, server‑less 3‑D terrain map powered by a single PMTiles file! 🎉

Thinking: We need to answer user question: they have a map showing bus positions updating every 30 seconds from PostGIS. Should they use PMTiles? Provide reasoning, explain that PMTiles is static, not suitable for dynamic data, suggest alternatives like vector tile server, using MapLibre with vector tiles from a tile server, maybe using Martin, tileserver-gl, or custom tile endpoint that queries PostGIS. Could also use GeoJSON source with periodic fetch, or use WebSocket. Provide guidance on architecture, pros/cons, and maybe hybrid approach: static basemap via PMTiles, dynamic bus positions via separate source (GeoJSON or vector tiles). Provide steps. Also reference skill. Provide final answer.

**Short answer:** No – PMTiles is a *static* archive (one file that never changes). It’s perfect for basemap data that you generate once and then serve from a CDN, but it can’t be used for data that changes every few seconds, like live bus positions.  

Below is a quick decision‑tree, a short‑list of better options, and a concrete example of how you can combine a static PMTiles basemap with a live‑data source so you still get the benefits of PMTiles for the background map while keeping the bus layer dynamic.

---

## 1️⃣ Why PMTiles Won’t Work for Live Bus Positions

| Feature | PMTiles | Live bus positions (30 s updates) |
|---------|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **File content** | Fixed at creation time. Adding or removing a tile means rewriting the whole archive (or at least a large chunk). | Needs to reflect the current state of the fleet every 30 s. |
| **Update mechanism** | You would have to rebuild the archive, upload a new file, and bust caches – far too heavy for a 30‑second cadence. | You want a lightweight request (JSON, protobuf, or vector‑tile query) that returns only the latest points. |
| **Client‑side behavior** | MapLibre reads the archive via HTTP **Range** requests; the client never asks the server for “the latest version”. | The client must ask the server for the *current* data each refresh. |
| **Typical use‑case** | Basemap, terrain, static raster imagery, historic datasets, pre‑rendered vector tiles. | Real‑time vehicle tracking, sensor streams, live alerts, any data that changes frequently. |

Because PMTiles is immutable, you would end up rebuilding the whole file every 30 seconds, uploading it to S3 (or another static host), and waiting for the CDN to propagate – a process that would take minutes, not seconds.  

**Conclusion:** PMTiles is not the right tool for the *bus‑position* layer.

---

## 2️⃣ Recommended Architecture for a Live‑Vehicle Map

| Layer | Recommended source type | Why it fits |
|------|------------------------|-------------|
| **Basemap (roads, landuse, labels, terrain)** | **PMTiles** (static) | One‑file, CDN‑cached, no server needed. |
| **Bus positions (dynamic)** | **Vector‑tile endpoint** *or* **GeoJSON/TopoJSON** fetched every 30 s | Can be generated on‑the‑fly from PostGIS, returns only the features that changed. |
| **Optional: Labels / icons** | Same dynamic source (vector tiles) or separate GeoJSON | Keeps styling consistent. |

### Two common ways to serve the live data

| Option | How it works | Typical stack |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| **A. Vector‑tile endpoint (recommended for many points)** | A tiny tile server (e.g. **Martin**, **tileserver-gl**, **t-rex**, **tessera**, **pg_tileserv**, **tiledb**, **GeoServer**, **MapServer**) reads the PostGIS table, builds vector tiles on demand, and returns them in MapLibre’s *pbf* format (`application/x-protobuf`). | • **PostGIS** → **pg_tileserv** (or **t-rex**) → `https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`<br>• Register the protocol with MapLibre (no extra library needed – MapLibre already knows how to fetch `url`‑based vector tiles). |
| **B. GeoJSON polling** | Every 30 s the client does a `GET /api/bus‑positions?bbox=…` (or a simple `/api/bus‑positions`) that returns a FeatureCollection of the latest points. | • **Express / FastAPI / Flask** → query PostGIS → `res.json(geojson)`<br>• In MapLibre use a `geojson` source and call `source.setData(newGeojson)` on each poll. |
| **C. WebSocket / Server‑Sent Events** (more advanced) | Server pushes updates as soon as they happen; client updates the source without polling. | • **Node.js + ws**, **Python + websockets**, **PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY** → push new points. |

**Why option A (vector tiles) is often the best for a fleet of buses:**

* It scales to thousands of points without blowing up the payload (tiles are compressed protobufs).  
* You can keep the same styling logic you already use for vector basemaps (e.g. `type: "circle"` with `paint` rules).  
* The tile server can be *stateless* – it just reads the DB on each request, no need to keep a cache of the whole fleet.  
* You can still use the same MapLibre style JSON for both the static PMTiles basemap and the dynamic vector‑tile source.

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal Working Example

Below is a **complete, copy‑paste‑able** snippet that shows:

1. **Static basemap** from a PMTiles file (hosted on S3, Cloudflare R2, or GitHub Pages).  
2. **Live bus layer** served as vector tiles from a tiny `pg_tileserv` instance (or any other tile server).  
3. **Automatic refresh** every 30 seconds (you can also rely on the tile server’s caching headers).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Live Bus Map (PMTiles + Vector Tiles)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.2.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Register the PMTiles protocol (once per page)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Create the map with a static PMTiles basemap
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- static basemap (vector) ----
        basemap: {
          type: 'vector',
          // NOTE: the URL can be a plain HTTPS link to a .pmtiles file.
          // The protocol handler will turn it into a TileJSON on‑the‑fly.
          url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_STATIC_HOST/basemap.pmtiles'
        },
        // ---- live bus positions (vector tiles) ----
        buses: {
          type: 'vector',
          // Example endpoint from pg_tileserv (or any tile server)
          // The server should respect the `Cache-Control: no-store` header
          // or set a very short max‑age (e.g. 30s) so the client refetches.
          url: 'https://YOUR_TILE_SERVER/bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // ---- draw the basemap (you can keep the default style from the PMTiles file) ----
        // Example: water layer from the PMTiles source
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'basemap',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        // ---- draw the bus positions ----
        {
          id: 'bus-points',
          type: 'circle',
          source: 'buses',
          'source-layer': 'bus_positions',   // <-- name inside the tile server's TileJSON
          paint: {
            'circle-radius': [
              'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
              12, 4,
              16, 8
            ],
            'circle-color': '#ff6600',
            'circle-stroke-width': 1,
            'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.33, 47.60], // Seattle (example)
    zoom: 12
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Force a refresh of the bus tiles every 30 seconds
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // The tile server should already send a short max‑age, but we can
  // also manually invalidate the source to be safe.
  const REFRESH_MS = 30_000;
  setInterval(() => {
    // This tells MapLibre to re‑request tiles for the source.
    // It does *not* reload the whole style, only the source.
    map.getSource('buses').setTiles(
      // Re‑write the URL template to bust the browser cache.
      // Adding a dummy query param forces a new request.
      [`https://YOUR_TILE_SERVER/bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?ts=${Date.now()}`]
    );
  }, REFRESH_MS);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What you need to replace

| Placeholder | What to put |
|-------------|--------------|
| `https://YOUR_STATIC_HOST/basemap.pmtiles` | URL of the **static** PMTiles basemap (e.g. `https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/europe.pmtiles`). |
| `https://YOUR_TILE_SERVER/bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` | URL of a **vector‑tile server** that reads the `bus_positions` table from PostGIS. If you use **pg_tileserv**, the endpoint looks like `https://my‑tiles.example.com/bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`. |
| `bus_positions` (source‑layer) | The name of the layer inside the vector‑tile TileJSON. With pg_tileserv you can set it with `?layer=bus_positions` or it defaults to the table name. |
| `center` / `zoom` | Your initial view. |

